\n\nHmm, maybe put in config_level. I'll add a components entry for flow straighteners with characteristics noting applicable models.\n\nAlso note: FMT-Dc consists of two FMT-Lx cartridges in one housing — that's a composition note; could add to component characteristics or description.\n\nModel ids mentioned: FMT-L, FMT-S, FMT-Lx, FMT-Dc. Should I include model_family? The fragment references these models; but the main matrix was likely on earlier pages. The instruction says extract content present in THIS fragment only. The models are mentioned; including model_family with these model ids seems reasonable but attributes unknown. Maybe safer to include model ids in a models list? The fragment mentions them in installation conditions. I think including model_family with the four model ids is defensible, but the rules say include only if cert covers >1 model variant — it does. But since this fragment is part 3 of 3 and earlier parts presumably had the models, should I re-extract? The instruction: \"Return one JSON object conforming to the layered schema for the content present in THIS fragment only.\" So include what's present. I'll include model_family with models FMT-L, FMT-S, FMT-Lx, FMT-Dc since they're mentioned, but attributes{} empty. Hmm, that might conflict with merge. I think it's fine to include.\n\nActually, maybe better not to fabricate a model_family structure not fully present. But the models are named. I'll include with just model_id entries.\n\nCertificate fields:\n- number: \"R137/2012-B-NL1-18.05\"\n- scheme: \"B\"\n- project_number: \"1901860\"\n- page_total: 5\n- member_state: \"The Netherlands\"\n\nIssuing authority: NMi — the logo says NMi. person info not present. I could include issuing_authority with name \"NMi\" and oiml_issuer_id maybe \"NL1\"? The number contains \"NL1\" which likely is the issuer id (NMi). Not certain; leave out oiml_issuer_id. I'll include issuing_authority.name = \"NMi\". Address unknown — omit.\n\nRecommendation: R137